DB(1)                          P32W Unix                           DB(1)

NAME
       db - debugger

SYNOPSIS
       db program [arg ...]

DESCRIPTION
       db runs program with the given arguments under its control,
       stopped before its first instruction, and reads commands
       from the standard input after the prompt db>. program must be
       a path (./prog, /bin/ls): the PATH is not searched. The program
       shares the terminal with db.

       db works at the machine level: it shows instructions, not C
       source. It reads the symbol table that ld(1) leaves at the end
       of an a.out, so places can be given by name; for a program
       linked with ld -s, use addresses. On starting it says how many
       symbols it found and shows the first instruction.

       Instructions are shown as

              0x00001034 <main+16>            addi   r1, r0, 10

       the address, the nearest text symbol at or below it, and the
       disassembled instruction (branch and jump targets with their
       symbol). A word that is no instruction is shown as .word.

       A where is a symbol (main), a symbol plus an offset
       (main+0x20), or a number (0x1000, or decimal). A C name that
       is a register name (sp, r1) is found as _C_name too. Global
       symbols are preferred to local ones of the same name.

       When the program stops on a signal other than a trap, db
       prints signal N; the signal is delivered when the program
       goes on. When the program exits db prints its status (or the
       signal that killed it); after that only bl, sym and q work.

COMMANDS
       c      continue until a breakpoint, a signal or the end.
       s [n]  step n instructions (default 1), into calls.
       n [n]  step n instructions, running each CALL or CALLR
              through to the instruction after it.
       f      finish: run until the current function returns. db
              finds the return address through the frame pointer,
              or, in a function's first three instructions (before
              its prologue push r14; mov r14, sp has made the frame),
              on the stack; that case needs the program's symbols.
       b where
              set a breakpoint at where (rounded down to a multiple
              of 4); db prints its number. At most 32.
       d [n]  delete breakpoint n; without n, all of them.
       bl     list the breakpoints.
       r      show the registers r1-r15 and the pc.
       set reg value
              set a register: r1-r15, sp, fp (r14) or pc. value is
              a where.
       x where [n]
              show n words of memory (default 4), four to a line, in
              hex. where is rounded down to a multiple of 4.
       p sym  show the address of sym and the word there, in hex and
              in decimal.
       l [where] [n]
              disassemble n instructions (default 8) from where
              (default the pc). => marks the pc, * a breakpoint.
       bt     backtrace: the current function, then the return
              address of each frame along the frame-pointer chain
              (r14), at most 32 deep.
       sym [prefix]
              list the symbols (all, or those beginning with prefix):
              address, type letter (T D B A, lower case if local) and
              name.
       q      quit; the program is killed. End of file does the same.

       An empty line repeats the last s or n (with its count), or
       continues the last l from where it ended. Any other command
       prints the list of commands.

       Breakpoints are BREAK instructions, written into the
       program's own copy of its text only while it runs. Stepping
       puts temporary breakpoints wherever the instruction can go
       next.

EXAMPLES
       $ db ./prog file
       db: ./prog, 212 symbols; stopped at the start
       0x00001000 <_start>             lw     r1, 0(sp)
       db> b main
       breakpoint 1 at 0x00001024 <main>
       db> c
       stopped: 0x00001024 <main>               addi   sp, sp, -4
       db> n 5
       db> bt
       db> x buf 8
       db> q

       (the output of n, bt and x is not shown.)

EXIT STATUS
       0 after q or end of file; 1 if the program could not be
       started; 2 for a usage error.

SEE ALSO
       cc(1), as(1), ld(1), ptrace(2), a.out(5)

BUGS
       There is no source-level debugging: no line numbers, C types
       or local variable names (cc emits none). Values are words; x
       shows only hex words, and there is no way to write memory.
       set takes no negative numbers.

       f and bt rely on the frame layout cc makes; in hand-written
       assembly, or in a function's first three instructions when
       there are no symbols, they may be wrong. bt stops at a frame
       pointer outside the 8 MiB user address space.

       Command lines are at most 255 characters. There are no
       watchpoints, conditional breakpoints or attach to a running
       process; a program cannot be restarted without leaving db.
